    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [X] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.map\n\n### Description\n\nI am having trouble coming up with a consistent way to automatically open/close the minimap depending on the buffer type.\r\n\r\nSpecifically, initially I had an autocmd for `BufEnter` as such:\r\n\r\n```lua\r\nfunction()\r\n    local enable = {\r\n        [\"nix\"] = true,\r\n        [\"rust\"] = true,\r\n        [\"lua\"] = true,\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    print(vim.bo.filetype)\r\n    if enable[vim.bo.filetype] then\r\n        MiniMap.open()\r\n    else\r\n        MiniMap.close()\r\n    end\r\nend\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe `print` in there is because I noticed whenever I opened a hover window (e.g. using `vim.lsp.buf.hover()`), the minimap would close because apparently that triggers `BufEnter`, even though the cursor hasn't left the original buffer yet, and the buffer type for such a hover window is empty.\r\n\r\nWould you have any guidance on how to best tackle this problem with the least number of surprises but also in a way in which I always have the minimap available in a list of buffer types, but have it close when I switch to other buffer types, without it closing if I move into a hover window?",
